#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ea56b4-2066-5aaf-aea9-08515803fc0b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression With Parentheses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c5ab47-14e9-5e2b-9447-8973112061ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01c5ab47-14e9-5e2b-9447-8973112061ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484819} :answer-field/id #uuid "35121271-51e1-5687-872f-b1f42af21c50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3cfd772-56f0-5f8d-9613-e2a636e3f6c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-133802" :question/problem "Aaron, Beatrice, and Chetna have each baked $2$ cherry pies and $3$ apple pies for the sale. In total, they baked{{field-1}}\npies." :question/worked-solution "We need to write and evaluate an expression representing the total number of pies.\n\nSince each of them baked $2$ cherry pies and $3$ apple pies, the number of pies each person made is:\n\n$$\n{{2}_{⏟}}_{\\text{cherry pies}}+{{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{apple pies}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person made this number of pies, and there are $3$ people, we must multiply the number of pies by $3.$ Therefore, the total number of pies is:\n\n$$\n{{(2+3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+3)\\times 3 & =(2+3)\\times 3 \\\\  & =5\\times 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n5\\times 3=15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85f7df01-47df-5ca6-bdc2-9485017b51bd" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Two-Digit Number by a Two-Digit Number: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5de1019-e44b-51c8-b1d6-811e595007cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5de1019-e44b-51c8-b1d6-811e595007cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485342} :answer-field/id #uuid "fac530a4-31cf-5ed3-9ace-18c2b43e1fd3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dffbb18-69db-5548-9e20-8e99e6ab3d7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-133803" :question/problem "At the bake sale, there are $12$ tables with $18$ cakes at each table. Therefore, there are{{field-1}}\ncakes in total." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total number of cakes, we need to multiply $12$ by $18.$\n\nSo, we write it this way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}12\\times 8=96\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}12\\times 10=120\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}96+120=216\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12\\times 18=216.$\n\nTherefore, there are $216$ cakes in total."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a1e476-39d5-529d-8632-c5dce93b8ed7" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing When the Hundreds Digit Is Less Than the Divisor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ef72a28-0a22-52df-87fd-e897740c3fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ef72a28-0a22-52df-87fd-e897740c3fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485028} :answer-field/id #uuid "9df461e3-1782-571c-a80a-bda5b40ea94b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43bd5eed-55d7-5512-b491-efa9bc5b652f" :question/math-academy-id "q-133804" :question/problem "If a total of $9$ people made all the cakes for the sale, and each person made the same number of cakes, then they each made{{field-1}}\ncakes." :question/worked-solution "We know that there are $216$ cakes in total. So, each person made $216÷9$ cakes.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{2\\,\\phantom{|}1\\,\\phantom{|}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that $2$ (the number of hundreds in $216$) is less than the divisor $9.$ Therefore, we should consider both the hundreds and tens:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{2\\,\\phantom{|}1\\,\\phantom{|}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$↓$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $21÷9=2\\,\\text{R}\\,3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over tens: $2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $9\\times 2=18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $21-18=3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Bring down: $6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{2\\,\\phantom{|}1\\,\\phantom{|}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\boxed{\\text{0}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $36÷9=4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over ones: $4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $9\\times 4=36$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $36-36=0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe get the remainder $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n216÷9=24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7bb96c-c682-54da-98d7-aa1b6960ee9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Improper Fraction and a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8784ea2-9999-5a27-973c-a5d014b476bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8784ea2-9999-5a27-973c-a5d014b476bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485307} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e6b9edd-f6dd-500c-8e8b-88adbe16a5bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb3049fb-d3cf-5bb1-b456-516f986d2262" :question/math-academy-id "q-133805" :question/problem "Darryl buys $2$ cherry pies and $\\frac{4}{3}$ of an apple pie. How much pie did Darryl buy in total? Express your final answer as a mixed number.\n\nDarryl bought\n{{field-1}}\npies." :question/worked-solution "To find out how much pie Darryl bought, we have to calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n2+\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add $2$ and $\\frac{4}{3},$ we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $3.$\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $3$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 3}{1\\times 3}=\\frac{6}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{6+4}{3}=\\frac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as a mixed number, we have\n\n$$\n10÷3=3\\,\\text{R}\\,1=3\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Darryl bought $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ pies."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ee87e73-843c-5596-b1f2-341d4e7266f8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Final Answer to a Mixed Number: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0910b7ee-a05f-5aa4-98df-c9e93c234cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484832} {:answer/id #uuid "b97ecb8d-3184-5f67-a554-c761f886d4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b97ecb8d-3184-5f67-a554-c761f886d4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508668} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe34f7c-29fe-5043-b266-5728c91eaf26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa1b20cc-21de-5821-9525-46da1d460229" :question/math-academy-id "q-133806" :question/problem "Of the $2$ of Darryl's cherry pies, Ellen takes $\\frac{5}{7}$ of a pie. As a mixed number, Darryl has{{field-1}}\ncherry pies left." :question/worked-solution "To find out how much cherry pie Darryl has left, we must calculate the difference $2-\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{5}{7}$ from $2,$ we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $7.$\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $7$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 7}{1\\times 7}=\\frac{14}{7}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{7}-\\frac{5}{7}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{7}-\\frac{5}{7}=\\frac{14-5}{7}=\\frac{9}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n9÷7=1\\,\\text{R}\\,2=1\\,\\frac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Darryl has $1\\,\\frac{2}{7}$ cherry pies left."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf381e5b-c6ba-5255-8d6a-797576f71baf" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Fractions When the Least Common Denominator Equals the Product of Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7a4f173-8537-52f7-8940-6a99ceeca293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7a4f173-8537-52f7-8940-6a99ceeca293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485246} :answer-field/id #uuid "c37e0a1c-7302-5dcb-9393-66c1caaf2c23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d6e4d0e-a01c-54ee-9889-0986a07f14e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "less" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d6e4d0e-a01c-54ee-9889-0986a07f14e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "less" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484845} :answer-field/id #uuid "d74ba4e3-6609-57f0-a5d9-9c0bee314a02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a57b3c9-03a4-52de-a46c-64a4643241ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-133807" :question/problem "After Ellen takes her bite of Darryl's cherry pie, Darryl's remaining cherry pie is{{field-1}}\nthan Darryl's remaining apple pie because  $1\\frac{2}{7}$\n{{field-2}} $\\frac{4}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Darryl has $1\\frac{2}{7}$ cherry pies and $\\frac{4}{3}$ apple pies remaining.\n\nWe cannot compare the fractions directly as they don't have a common denominator.\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $7\\times 3=21.$\n\nTo put $1\\frac{2}{7}$ over a denominator of $21,$ we first convert to an improper fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n1\\frac{2}{7}=\\frac{9}{7}=\\frac{9\\times 3}{7\\times 3}=\\frac{27}{21}\n$$\n\nTo put $\\frac{4}{3}$ over a denominator of $21,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{4\\times 7}{3\\times 7}=\\frac{28}{21}\n$$\n\nWe can now compare the fractions $\\frac{27}{21}$ and $\\frac{28}{21}$ by looking at the numerators only.\n\nSince $27<28,$ the correct statement must be:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{21}<\\frac{28}{21}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n\n> \"After Ellen takes her bite of Darryl's cherry pie, Darryl's remaining cherry pie is $<$ than Darryl's remaining apple pie because $1\\frac{2}{7}<\\frac{4}{3}.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "476cf433-d982-5f14-89be-48aea2abaf55" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6818a4d-d22e-50c1-a69c-bc909b29e84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6818a4d-d22e-50c1-a69c-bc909b29e84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485209} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb066ac7-3e74-53e0-a852-3680d4d4d5ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d63aa21-54c3-5793-bdf0-1f7c092e61d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-133808" :question/problem "Darryl gives $\\frac{2}{5}$ of his apple pies to his daughter. What fraction of a whole pie does Darryl's daughter have?\n\n*Remember that Darryl bought $\\frac{4}{3}$ of an apple pie.*\n\nDarryl's daughter has\n{{field-1}}\nof a pie." :question/worked-solution "We know that Darryl has $\\frac{4}{3}$ of an apple pie.\n\nTo calculate the fraction of a whole pie that Darryl's daughter has, we need to multiply $\\frac{2}{5}$ by $\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{2\\times 4}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{8}{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Darry's daughter have $\\frac{8}{15}$ of a pie."}]}
